From: rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] inputmethod: a user-forced keyboard that survives non-text-input surfaces

The on-screen keyboard is only ever raised for a surface that speaks the
Wayland text-input protocol. XWayland clients cannot speak it, so Java/X11
applications (Burp Suite, and every other XWayland window) get a text field, a
cursor and no keyboard. The DBus escape hatch forceActivate() exists but does
not persist: refreshActive() re-imposes the text-input gate and calls
setActive(false) on the next text-input signal, shouldShowOnActive() only shows
the panel for touch/tablet input, and setTrackedWindow() clears m_shouldShowPanel
on the next focus change -- so a forced keyboard is torn down within a moment of
appearing.

This adds one member, m_forcedActive, set by forceActivate() and cleared when
the user dismisses the keyboard (hide()). While it is set, refreshActive() keeps
the keyboard active, shouldShowOnActive() shows it regardless of the input
device (short of VirtualKeyboardVisibility::Never), and setTrackedWindow() keeps
the panel up and re-shows it across focus changes.

The keystroke delivery path already exists on KWin 6.7 and needed nothing here:
plasma-keyboard is an input method (zwp_input_method_v1) that sends commit_string
/ keysym rather than keycodes, and InputMethod::commitString()'s final else branch
already converts committed text to keysyms and injects them into the seat keyboard
via forwardKeySym()/notifyKeyboardKey() when the focused surface has no text-input
-- which is exactly the XWayland case. So once this patch lets the panel be shown
over an XWayland window, the keys land in it. (On older KWin without that else
branch, a separate change would be needed; this is why the port measured the KWin
version first.)

Against KWin 6.7.2 (Kali kwin 6.7.2-1; src/inputmethod.cpp, src/inputmethod.h).
The hunks are anchored on stable API and apply with fuzz on neighbouring 6.7.x.
---
diff --git a/src/inputmethod.cpp b/src/inputmethod.cpp
index 4f3cd3f..3b1b855 100644
--- a/src/inputmethod.cpp
+++ b/src/inputmethod.cpp
@@ -161,6 +161,9 @@ void InputMethod::show()
 
 void InputMethod::hide()
 {
+    // A user dismissing the keyboard clears the forced-on state; the next
+    // text-input-driven surface decides on its own whether to raise it again.
+    m_forcedActive = false;
     m_shouldShowPanel = false;
     if (m_panel) {
         m_panel->hide();
@@ -170,6 +173,12 @@ void InputMethod::hide()
 
 bool InputMethod::shouldShowOnActive() const
 {
+    // A user-forced keyboard (forceActivate over an XWayland / non-text-input
+    // surface) is driven by mouse or keyboard, so NonMouseInput would hide it.
+    // Show it anyway while the force is in effect.
+    if (m_forcedActive) {
+        return m_virtualKeyboardVisibility != VirtualKeyboardVisibility::Never;
+    }
     switch (m_virtualKeyboardVisibility) {
     case VirtualKeyboardVisibility::Never:
         return false;
@@ -188,6 +197,15 @@ void InputMethod::refreshActive()
     auto t2 = seat->textInputV2();
     auto t3 = seat->textInputV3();
 
+    // If the user forced the keyboard on (e.g. over an XWayland window that
+    // cannot speak text-input), keep it active regardless of what the focused
+    // surface supports. Without this, any text-input enabled-changed signal
+    // would call setActive(false) and tear the forced keyboard back down.
+    if (m_forcedActive) {
+        setActive(true);
+        return;
+    }
+
     bool active = false;
     if (auto focusedSurface = seat->focusedTextInputSurface()) {
         auto client = focusedSurface->client();
@@ -319,12 +337,20 @@ void InputMethod::setTrackedWindow(Window *trackedWindow)
         disconnect(m_trackedWindow, &Window::frameGeometryChanged, this, &InputMethod::cursorRectangleChanged);
     }
     m_trackedWindow = trackedWindow;
-    m_shouldShowPanel = false;
+    // A focus change normally hides the panel (m_shouldShowPanel = false). Keep
+    // a user-forced keyboard up across focus changes so it does not vanish the
+    // instant the pointer moves to another window.
+    if (!m_forcedActive) {
+        m_shouldShowPanel = false;
+    }
     if (m_trackedWindow) {
         connect(m_trackedWindow, &Window::frameGeometryChanged, this, &InputMethod::updateInputPanelState, Qt::QueuedConnection);
         connect(m_trackedWindow, &Window::frameGeometryChanged, this, &InputMethod::cursorRectangleChanged);
     }
     updateInputPanelState();
+    if (m_forcedActive) {
+        show();
+    }
     Q_EMIT activeWindowChanged();
 }
 
@@ -982,6 +1008,7 @@ bool InputMethod::activeClientSupportsTextInput() const
 
 void InputMethod::forceActivate()
 {
+    m_forcedActive = true;
     setActive(true);
     show();
 }
diff --git a/src/inputmethod.h b/src/inputmethod.h
index fa6806b..cc7039b 100644
--- a/src/inputmethod.h
+++ b/src/inputmethod.h
@@ -166,6 +166,15 @@ private:
     bool m_hasPendingModifiers = false;
     bool m_activeClientSupportsTextInput = false;
     bool m_shouldShowPanel = false;
+    // Set by forceActivate() (the DBus "show keyboard" entry point), cleared
+    // when the user dismisses the keyboard via hide(). While set, the text-input
+    // gate in refreshActive() is bypassed, shouldShowOnActive() returns true and
+    // focus changes do not drop the panel -- which is what lets the keyboard be
+    // raised over an XWayland / Java window that cannot speak text-input. On
+    // KWin 6.7 the delivery path already exists: commitString() synthesises key
+    // events into the seat keyboard for surfaces with no text-input, so the
+    // keys reach XWayland once the panel is allowed to show.
+    bool m_forcedActive = false;
 };
 
 }
